{"created": "2026-10-31T09:27:57.082210+00:00", "modified": "2026-11-14T09:54:47.756904+00:00", "id": 23833498, "conceptrecid": "22938355", "doi": "10.5281/zenodo.23833498", "conceptdoi": "10.5281/zenodo.22938355", "doi_url": "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23833498", "metadata": {"title": "Replication Package: Who Owns Granularity? Brand Data, Decision Rights, and Interdepartmental Conflict in Multinational Consumer Goods Firms", "doi": "10.5281/zenodo.23833498", "publication_date": "2026-10-31", "description": "<p>This package reproduces every number and every figure reported in the article, which models the conflict between marketing, legal and IT departments over the level of detail at which brand data are retained in multinational fast-moving consumer goods firms. The article formalises the conflict as a non-cooperative game, shows that aggregation is a strictly dominant strategy for the department that operates the platform, and evaluates the value of reassigning that decision right by Monte Carlo simulation across two firm archetypes differing ninety-fold in size.</p>\n<p><strong>Contents</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>archetypes.py</code> &mdash; model specification, Monte Carlo, premium decomposition, mechanism comparison, scale curve, complexity elasticity sweep</li>\n<li><code>extended_analyses.py</code> &mdash; tail statistics, corner-solution frontier, break-even coverage curve, full-range sensitivity</li>\n<li><code>robustness_analyses.py</code> &mdash; joint sensitivity in the two unidentified parameters, decomposition of the governance channels, the counter-channel in which retaining resolution raises rather than lowers compliance exposure, additional data-consuming functions, maturity-grid boundary diagnostic, identification of the revenue elasticity of resolution including the case in which the resolution&ndash;revenue link is removed entirely</li>\n<li><code>archetype_figures.py</code>, <code>extended_figures.py</code> &mdash; the eight figures at 600 dpi</li>\n<li><code>verify.py</code> &mdash; checks regenerated output against the values printed in the article</li>\n<li><code>archetype_results.json</code>, <code>extended_results.json</code>, <code>robustness_results.json</code> &mdash; machine-readable output</li>\n<li><code>figures/</code> &mdash; Figures 1&ndash;8 as published</li>\n<li><code>requirements.txt</code>, <code>Makefile</code>, <code>REPRODUCIBILITY.md</code></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Reproducing the results</strong></p>\n<p><code>pip install -r requirements.txt</code>, then <code>make all</code> regenerates everything from source (approximately 25 minutes on a single core), and <code>make verify</code> compares the output against the published figures and exits non-zero on any mismatch.</p>\n<p><strong>Determinism</strong></p>\n<p>The random seed is fixed at 20260823 and set through NumPy's <code>default_rng</code> (PCG64), which is reproducible across platforms for a given NumPy major version. Every reported statistic is a deterministic function of the code and the seed; no result depends on wall-clock time, process identifiers, thread count or hardware. All coordination arrangements are evaluated on paired draws &mdash; the same 100,000 parameter draws and the same uniform draw governing the compliance failure event &mdash; so the per-iteration difference is interpretable as a treatment effect within the model.</p>\n<p><strong>Data</strong></p>\n<p>No third-party, proprietary or human-subject data enter the analysis at any point, so no access request or data-use agreement is required to replicate. Every calibration anchor is a ratio disclosed in the published annual accounts of a large listed consumer goods multinational, a published industry percentage benchmark, a statutory instrument, or a parameter stated in the article and swept across its full plausible range. Appendix B of the article classifies every parameter by evidentiary status, including the two that no source identifies.</p>\n<p